From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 12:39:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Order FS work once per firmware run
 behind queued FS

Every render writes a Stamp barrier on the FS ring ahead of its FS
work, plus its own FS submit record and FS doorbell. The barrier holds
the FS queue until the render's TA has finished. On this firmware it is
required: with the FS barrier dropped an FS has started before its own
TA ended, and a render at depth 1 faulted the firmware.

A hypervisor trace of macOS's render queues on the same machine shows
no barrier work in 1693 work buffers and one submit record per render.
The firmware runs renders in runs of four (class 2, eight starts). So
at depth 64 we pay four FS barriers, eight submit records and four FS
doorbells per run where macOS pays no barrier and four records.

Add g16_render_fs_run (runtime 0644, default 0). With it set, a render
published behind running flights and behind at least one run of FS
work that is exposed and not yet finished (read from the FS stamp)
joins a batch of at most one run. The first render of the batch writes
its barrier entry, the later ones only their FS work. The FS WRITE
cursor, the one FS submit record and the one FS doorbell follow at the
end of the publish pass, after the opening barrier is re-encoded to
wait for the newest render's TA target. TA targets rise with the draw
ordinal and the TA ring is consumed in order, so when that barrier
passes every TA of the batch has finished. Per run at depth 64 this is
one barrier, five records and one FS doorbell.

A render with less than one run of FS work ahead first exposes any
open batch, then publishes with its own barrier, record and doorbell,
as before. An earlier form batched every render of a pass. On the
device it read 2.7% better at depth 64 and 4.3% worse at depth 8. Both
costs of a batch, the exposure at the end of the pass and the wait for
the newest TA, are hidden behind queued FS work at depth 64. At depth 8
the FS queue drains between refills, so they idle the FS. With the
gate, a shallow queue keeps the per-render form and gains no wait;
depth 1 never batches.

The rules are pure functions in fs_batch.rs. Host tests run a ring
model in which TAs finish in order and the FS queue consumes entries up
to WRITE; every mix of batched and per-render publications over ten
renders orders each FS behind its TA and writes at most one barrier
per render. The stats file gains a render_fs_run line: renders that
batched, renders that kept their own barrier, and a histogram of the
FS work found queued at each decision.
